WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.174
This camera has no lens.

2
00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:07.093
No glass. No battery. No sensor.

3
00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.451
Just a hole, a quarter of a millimetre wide.

4
00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:15.019
So how does a hole make a picture?

5
00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:25.734
Hold a piece of film up to a tree. Light from each point of the tree lands on every part of the film.

6
00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:35.990
So every part of the film sees the whole tree at once. All the light is there. The picture is not.

7
00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:43.926
Now put a wall with a small hole between them. Most of the light is stopped.

8
00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:54.001
Each part of the film can see only one point of the tree: the one in line with the hole. That is all a picture is.

9
00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:07.142
It is upside down, and left and right are swapped, because the rays cross in the hole. Move a point up the tree and its picture moves down.

10
00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:19.973
People have known this for about 2,400 years. For most of them, nobody could make the picture stay.

11
00:01:20.273 --> 00:01:32.478
c. 400 BC. Mozi and his school, China. The oldest account that survives: light through a small hole makes an upside-down picture, because light travels in straight lines.

12
00:01:32.778 --> 00:01:46.359
c. 350 BC. Aristotle, or a follower, Greece. The book Problems asks why the spots of sunlight under a tree turn into crescents during an eclipse. Every gap between the leaves is a pinhole.

13
00:01:47.683 --> 00:01:58.812
c. 1021. Ibn al-Haytham. In his Book of Optics he tests it. Several lamps, one hole, a spot of light for each. Screen one lamp and only its spot goes out.

14
00:01:59.417 --> 00:02:06.188
How big should the hole be? A big hole is bright, but each point of the scene lands as a disc the size of the hole.

15
00:02:07.117 --> 00:02:11.854
Make it smaller and the picture gets sharper, and dimmer: the exposure gets longer.

16
00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:28.281
For this camera, 28.7 mm from hole to film, the sharpest hole is about a quarter of a millimetre.

17
00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:34.147
Smaller still and it gets worse again. Light spreads out as it squeezes through a tiny hole.

18
00:02:37.781 --> 00:02:44.737
So a pinhole has a best size. The picture can never be sharper than that, which is why a lens was worth inventing.

19
00:02:47.383 --> 00:02:56.961
1544. Gemma Frisius, Leuven. He watches an eclipse of the sun safely inside a dark room, and prints a picture of the room the year after.

20
00:02:57.261 --> 00:03:10.540
1550 to 1568. Cardano, then Barbaro, Italy. Glass goes in the hole: first a glass disc, then a lens with a stop in front. The picture gets far brighter. That is lesson 2.

21
00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:19.732
1604. Johannes Kepler. He is the first to use the name camera obscura: Latin for dark room. Our word camera simply means room.

22
00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:29.305
The price of sharpness is light. A hole a quarter of a millimetre wide passes about seventy times less light than a hole of two millimetres.

23
00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:38.652
So the camera stands on something steady. You lift the slide, count, and push it down. That count is the exposure.

24
00:03:47.050 --> 00:04:01.135
1826 or 1827. Nicéphore Niépce, France. He puts a light-sensitive plate where the picture falls and waits: eight hours by tradition, perhaps days. It is the oldest camera photograph that survives.

26
00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:19.068
No lens, no shutter, no dial. Take a picture with nothing but a hole. The lens comes next, and you will know what it is for.

27
00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:29.314
So how does a hole make a picture? It lets each part of the film see only one point of the world.

28
00:04:29.614 --> 00:04:36.611
Look. On a sunny day, stand under a leafy tree. Every round spot of light on the ground is a picture of the sun.

29
00:04:36.911 --> 00:04:43.551
Try. A box, a pin, a piece of tracing paper. Point the hole at a window and look at the paper from behind.

30
00:04:43.851 --> 00:04:49.028
Next, lesson two: why a lens? The same picture, far brighter, and what it costs.

31
00:04:49.351 --> 00:04:55.678
Every picture in this film is drawn or simulated from the model. The camera has not been printed yet.
